      en: "Osu Castle, also known as Christiansborg Castle, is a historic fortification in Accra, Ghana, that has served as a center of political power for centuries. Built by the Danes in 1661, it was the principal outpost for Danish trade in gold and enslaved people along the West African coast. The castle's history is marked by frequent changes in ownership, including periods under Portuguese control and a notable takeover by the Akwamu people in 1693. In 1850, the British purchased the castle from Denmark, eventually converting it into the seat of the colonial governor. Following Ghana's independence in 1957, it became the official residence and office of the President, a role it maintained until 2013 when the executive seat moved to Jubilee House. Despite its somber history as a slave trade hub, Osu Castle remains a vital symbol of Ghana's transition from a colony to a sovereign nation. It stands prominently on the Atlantic shore, reflecting the architectural and historical layers of the nation's capital.",
